---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="02-conditionals"
  lessonSlug="01-letter-grade"
  hook="Same elif chain as the example — but with five branches and an order-of-branches trap that bites almost everyone the first time."
>
  <h2>Specification</h2>
  <p>Implement <code>letter_grade(score)</code>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>score &gt;= 90</code> → <code>"A"</code></li>
    <li><code>score &gt;= 80</code> → <code>"B"</code></li>
    <li><code>score &gt;= 70</code> → <code>"C"</code></li>
    <li><code>score &gt;= 60</code> → <code>"D"</code></li>
    <li><code>score &lt; 60</code> → <code>"F"</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The trap</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="What does this return for score = 95?  Predict first."
    code={`def letter_grade(score):
    if score >= 60:
        return "D"
    elif score >= 70:
        return "C"
    elif score >= 80:
        return "B"
    elif score >= 90:
        return "A"
    else:
        return "F"

print(letter_grade(95))
print(letter_grade(72))
print(letter_grade(50))`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Why this is wrong">
    <p>
      For <code>95</code>, the first branch (<code>&gt;= 60</code>) is already
      true → returns <code>"D"</code>. Python doesn't look ahead. From Think
      Python §5.5: <em>"Even if more than one condition is true, only the first
      true branch executes."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The fix: order high-to-low</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def letter_grade(score):
    if score >= 90:
        return "A"
    elif score >= 80:
        return "B"
    elif score >= 70:
        return "C"
    elif score >= 60:
        return "D"
    else:
        return "F"

# Boundary tests — these matter to the autograder:
for s in [95, 90, 89, 80, 70, 60, 59, 0]:
    print(f"letter_grade({s:>3}) = {letter_grade(s)}")`}
  />

  <Quiz
    question="With the BROKEN ordering above, letter_grade(95) returns:"
    choices={[
      { text: '"A"', explain: "If only — but the first branch (>= 60) is already true, so we never reach the >= 90 branch." },
      { text: '"B"', explain: "Same issue. We exit at the first matching elif." },
      { text: '"D"', correct: true, explain: "Right. The branches are tested in order and the first match returns. 95 >= 60 fires first." },
      { text: 'Error', explain: "The code runs fine — it just returns the wrong letter." },
    ]}
    hint="Read Think Python §5.5 again if this surprises you."
  />

  <h2>Now you try in WolfCode</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>01-letter-grade.py</code> and write the correct version. The
    autograder checks all 8 cases including <code>90</code>, <code>60</code>,
    and <code>59</code> (boundaries). The Tutor is enabled at L1–L3 — L4 is off
    by design.
  </p>
</Lecture>
